\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces $\mathrm  {FR}_Q$ vs $T$ at fixed thresholds. (a) Absolute $\tau $ on $\texttt  {sol\_Q\_scaled}$: the curves are nearly degenerate for any $\tau \in [0.2,\,1.0]$ because $q_{\qopname  \relax m{max}}\gg 1$ at $T\ge 100$; only $\tau _{\mathrm  {abs}}\gtrsim 3$ begins to clip the false mass. (b) Relative $\tau \cdot q_{\qopname  \relax m{max}}$: a single dimensionless cut at $\tau \approx 0.40$ matches or beats the classical FR at $T\ge 500$.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:absvsrel}{{4}{7}{$\FR _Q$ vs $T$ at fixed thresholds. (a) Absolute $\tau $ on $\texttt {sol\_Q\_scaled}$: the curves are nearly degenerate for any $\tau \in [0.2,\,1.0]$ because $q_{\max }\gg 1$ at $T\ge 100$; only $\tau _{\mathrm {abs}}\gtrsim 3$ begins to clip the false mass. (b) Relative $\tau \cdot q_{\max }$: a single dimensionless cut at $\tau \approx 0.40$ matches or beats the classical FR at $T\ge 500$}{figure.4}{}}
